@page
@model SimpleRadius.Pages.Backup.IndexModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Import / export";
}

<h2 class="mb-3">Import / export</h2>

<div class="row g-4">
    <div class="col-lg-6">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header">Export</div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p class="text-muted">
                    Download the full configuration — VLANs, clients, network access servers, SSID rules
                    and settings — as a single file. Keep it as a backup, or import it into another instance.
                </p>
                <form method="get">
                    <input type="hidden" name="handler" value="Export" />
                    <div class="form-check mb-3">
                        <input type="checkbox" class="form-check-input" id="accounting" name="accounting" value="true" />
                        <label class="form-check-label" for="accounting">Include accounting sessions</label>
                        <div class="form-text">Off by default. The session history can be large and is separate from the configuration.</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="d-flex gap-2">
                        <button type="submit" name="format" value="json" class="btn btn-primary">Download JSON</button>
                        <button type="submit" name="format" value="yaml" class="btn btn-primary">Download YAML</button>
                    </div>
                </form>
                <div class="alert alert-warning mt-3 mb-0 small">
                    The file contains your NAS <strong>shared secrets in plaintext</strong>. Store it
                    somewhere safe, as you would a password.
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-lg-6">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header">Import</div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p class="text-muted">
                    Load a JSON or YAML file. Records are matched by VLAN number, client identity, NAS
                    address and SSID: existing ones are updated, new ones are added. Nothing is deleted.
                </p>
                <form method="post" asp-page-handler="Import" enctype="multipart/form-data">
                    <div class="mb-3">
                        <label asp-for="Upload" class="form-label">Configuration file</label>
                        <input asp-for="Upload" type="file" class="form-control" accept=".json,.yaml,.yml,application/json,application/x-yaml" />
                    </div>
                    <div class="form-check mb-3">
                        <input asp-for="ImportSettings" class="form-check-input" />
                        <label asp-for="ImportSettings" class="form-check-label">Also import server settings</label>
                        <div class="form-text">
                            Off by default. When on, the file's default VLAN, reply attributes and other
                            settings overwrite the current ones.
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="form-check mb-3">
                        <input asp-for="ImportAccounting" class="form-check-input" />
                        <label asp-for="ImportAccounting" class="form-check-label">Also import accounting sessions</label>
                        <div class="form-text">
                            Off by default, and only does anything if the file was exported with accounting.
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary"
                            onclick="return confirm('Import this file? Existing records with the same key are overwritten.');">
                        Import
                    </button>
                </form>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<p class="text-muted small mt-4">
    The format is stable and human-editable: a client references its VLAN by number, so you can hand-write
    or template a configuration and import it. Internal ids are not included; accounting history is only
    present when you tick the box above.
</p>
